Chapter 28
Jiang Yu-han gives Qianxiang silk gauze in affection;
Xue Bao-chai wears red musk-scented beads in shy confusion

Now Lin Dai-yu had wrongly blamed Bao-yu for Skybright’s refusal to open the door the night before. The next day happened to be the day of farewell to the flowers. Her unspent anger was joined by sorrow at the passing of spring, and she had gathered some withered flowers and fallen petals to bury. Their fate reminded her irresistibly of her own. After weeping a little, she began to recite whatever lines came to her lips. She had no idea that Bao-yu was listening on the hillside. At first he merely nodded and sighed; but when he heard such lines as “Today I bury flowers, and people call me foolish;
In years to come, who knows who will bury me?” and “One day spring ends and beauty has grown old;
Flowers fall, people die, neither knowing of the other!”, he collapsed on the slope in an agony of grief, scattering the fallen flowers from the front of his robe. To think that Dai-yu, with her flower-like face and moon-like beauty, would one day be nowhere to be found: how could his heart not break? And if Dai-yu must one day vanish beyond all finding, so must Bao-chai, Caltrop, Aroma and the others. When Bao-chai and the rest could no longer be found, where would he himself be?

If he could not even tell where he would be, or where he would go, whose would this place be then, this garden, these flowers and willows? One thought led to another, and that to a third; he followed them round and round, until he scarcely knew what senseless thing he wished he might become, without a vestige of awareness, beyond creation’s reach and free of the world’s net, if only he could be released from this sorrow. As the lines say:
Flower-shadows never leave his side;
Birdsong sounds on either hand.
